  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAn increasingly sheared mid-level trough crossing the Missouri \nRiver Valley early this morning will align with a strong cold \nfront and departing upper-level jet streak to bring a gradually \nweakening band of rain from west to east across the area today. \nExpect the rain to last around four hours at a given location, \nwith a focus on the mid- morning to early afternoon for the \nChicago metro. Unseasonably warm temps this morning will fall \nthroughout the afternoon as blustery northwest winds develop \nbehind the cold front. \n\nA substantially colder and drier airmass will build over the \narea Monday in the wake of todays's cold front. Temperatures \nwill be seasonably cold for late November, with some areas \nacross interior northern Illinois possibly seeing lows in the \nupper teens Monday night. Though low-level flow will turn \nnorth/northeast Monday night into Tuesday, marginal SST-850 hPa \ndelta temps and a strong inversion around 6kft should limit lake\neffect precip to only some flurries or very light snow showers \ninto northwest Indiana and northeast Illinois. \n\nAn incredibly complex pattern will evolve across much of the \nCONUS midweek. A trio of waves extending from the southern \nCanadian prairies to northeast Mexico on Tuesday are progged to \ninteract, in some form, across the eastern CONUS late Wednesday \nthrough Thanksgiving, setting the stage for active weather over \na potentially large corridor east of the Mississippi River. \nUnsurprisingly, the ensemble spread of solutions is quite \nexpansive, with solutions ranging from little to no notable \nweather over our area to wet snow for at least a portion of the \nforecast area. The later solution would be the most impactful, \nwith an eventual congealment of the three waves occurring around\nthe Upper Ohio River Valley as dynamic cooling increases \nchances for mixed precip/snow. Will need to closely monitor this\nperiod, but the unique interaction between each of the waves \nbegets great uncertainty for at least a couple more days.\n\nAnother trough may bring precip, likely as rain, across the \nwestern Great Lakes next weekend, but confidence this far out is\nvery low given the unknown outcome of the midweek weather.\n\nKluber",
